  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "**Introduction/Context**  \nThe study compares proteome and glycoproteome profiles of intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs) and normal duct tissues (NDs) to identify differentially expressed proteins and glycopeptides. The glycoproteome analysis focuses on identifying glycopeptides with altered expression in IPMNs that lack corresponding changes in global protein levels.  \n\n**Keywords**  \nIPMN, glycoproteome, proteome, differential expression, biomarkers, pancreatic neoplasms.  \n\n**Candidate Genes**  \nANO9, ADAM10, CPD, TMEM63A, TSPAN13, EPHA2, MET, ERBB3.  \n\n**Pathway Enrichments**  \n**Glycoproteome-Only Differentially Regulated Genes (Proteome-Neutral)**  \n*Upregulated Glycopeptides:*  \n- **KEGG_2016 (UP):**  \n  | Theme | Direction | Leading Genes |  \n  |-------|-----------|---------------|  \n  | Cell adhesion molecules (CAMs) | Up | CPD, ERBB3, ICAM2 |  \n  | Focal adhesion | Up | MET, EPHA2, ITGA6 |  \n  | ECM-receptor interaction | Up | CPD, TSPAN13 |  \n\n*Downregulated Glycopeptides:*  \n- **KEGG_2016 (DOWN):**  \n  | Theme | Direction | Leading Genes |  \n  |-------|-----------|---------------|  \n  | Metabolic pathways | Down | GUSB, ATP1A3 |  \n\n**Targeted Subset Analysis**  \nGlycopeptides from **MET**, **EPHA2**, and **CPD** (key drivers of cancer progression) showed significant upregulation without global proteome changes. These genes are enriched in pathways linked to pancreatic cancer progression (e.g., focal adhesion, cell migration).  \n\n**Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)**  \n| Gene | Direction | Role/Function | Pathway(s) |  \n|-------|-----------|---------------|------------|  \n| **MET** | Up (glycopeptide) | Growth factor receptor | Focal adhesion, cancer progression |  \n| **EPHA2** | Up (glycopeptide) | Cell membrane organization | Cell adhesion, metastasis |  \n| **CPD** | Up (glycopeptide) | Extracellular matrix remodeling | ECM-receptor interaction |  \n\n**Comparisons**  \n- **Proteome vs. Glycoproteome:** 57 glycoproteins were differentially regulated at the glycopeptide level, but only 23 showed concordant changes in global proteome. 34 glycoproteins (e.g., **MET**, **TSPAN13**) showed glycosylation-specific alterations.  \n- **Discordant Findings:** **CPD** was upregulated in glycoproteome (log2FC: 2.22) but unchanged in proteome (log2FC: 0.18, FDR: 0.42), suggesting glycosylation-specific regulation.  \n\n**Implications**  \nGlycopeptides like **MET** and **EPHA2** may serve as IPMN-specific biomarkers with higher specificity than global protein expression. Glycosylation-driven alterations in cell adhesion pathways suggest novel mechanisms of IPMN progression.  \n\n**Next Steps & Clarification**  \n1. Validate glycosylation sites (e.g., **MET** N399/N410) in IPMN cyst fluids.  \n2. Functional assays to test the role of **CPD** glycosylation in ECM remodeling.  \n3. Compare glycoproteome profiles of IPMNs vs. invasive pancreatic cancers.  \n\n**References**  \nNot validated in this analysis.  \n\n---  \n**Analysis Notes**  \n- **Cell Adhesion/ECM Pathways:** Enriched in upregulated glycopeptides (e.g., **CPD**, **TSPAN13**), aligning with IPMN's cyst-forming and invasive potential.  \n- **Metabolic Downregulation:** Reduced glycosylation of metabolic enzymes (e.g., **GUSB**) suggests a shift toward tumor-promoting glycoforms.  \n- **Therapeutic Relevance:** **MET** and **EPHA2** glycopeptides may inform targeted therapies or early detection strategies.",
